      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm currently looking at the best way to catalog a new acquisition of rare 19th-century manuscripts for our university collection here in Belgium. Could you provide a comparison between the Dewey Decimal Classification (DDC) and the Library of Congress Classification (LCC) systems? I'm specifically interested in how they handle metadata, scalability for large collections, and the level of granularity they offer for specialized historical documents.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "While the Library of Congress Classification (LCC) offers exceptional granularity with dedicated subdivisions for historical manuscripts and rare books, the Dewey Decimal Classification (DDC) provides a simpler, decimal structure often better suited for general academic libraries with less specialized holdings. In terms of scalability, LCC's historically vast hierarchy prevents item fatigue and allows for deep specialization, whereas DDC can become unwieldy when applied to niche European archives despite its decade-by-decade expansion mechanisms. For your Belgian university's rare 19th-century manuscripts, the LCC is generally recommended due to its more robust metadata framework for unique items, though DDC remains viable if your collection emphasizes practical cataloging efficiency over extensive specialized distinctions.",
