    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Thanks for that. One thing I'm worried about is the sheer messiness of the current unprocessed state of the manuscripts. Some of these boxes are just a total chaos of loose papers, scraps, and mixed-up dates. If we go with LCC, how much work is it going to take to create a sense of order for items that are currently so disorganized and seemingly random?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Applying LCC to disorganized manuscripts requires a two-phase approach: first, a rigorous provenance study to assign a granular subject heading for each item's original context (which LCC excels at with its four-digit subdivisions), and second, a physical re-ordering process that drafters can automate because the system simplifies sorting criteria compared to free-text negotiations. The Granularity of LCC ensures that even mixed-up dates or varied formats are cataloged distinctly early, but the sheer volume of manual labor needed to verify these details and establish a consistent metadata schema will be significant. Ultimately, while the technical categorization is robust, the operational workload will be determined more by your internal staff's training and the time allocated for this \"clean-up\" phase than by the classification system itself. You might consider a pilot re-archiving of a single box type to test the workflow before full-scale migration.",
      "source": "target",
